WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.902 line:0%
-Hugh, it is great to see you,

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.638 line:0%
and thank you so much
for doing our show.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.940 line:0%
You were one of our first guests

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.877 line:0%
when we did our
quarantine show from my house,

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:15.815 line:0%
and we baked
challah bread together.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.049 line:0%
-We did.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.385 line:0%
I don't know about you,
but what was the reaction

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:21.253 line:0%
at your house from your challah?

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:24.557 line:0%
-Dude, it went crazy.
People loved it.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.358 line:0%
People freaked out
and they said, "You made this?"

00:00:26.358 --> 00:00:27.526 line:0%
And I go, "Oh, yeah."

00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:28.728 line:0%
They go, "But how --"

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:32.832 line:0%
It was the egg wash.
It was the secret move.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.569 line:0%
-It was weird, because I went
somewhere recently,

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.805 line:0%
like a long time after,
and this person looked at me.

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:41.007 line:0%
I was in the backyard.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:43.343 line:0%
It was one of those
social-distance things.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.677 line:0%
They said, "So, your challah..."

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.413 line:0%
and already, like,
looked a little annoyed with me.

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:50.583 line:0%
And I said, "Right.
For an Aussie, not bad, right?"

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:54.587 line:0%
And they went, "Mnh-mnh.
You should have 12 braids.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.223 line:0%
Like, religiously speaking."

00:00:57.223 --> 00:01:00.126 line:0%
And I was like, "Oh, no.
You don't understand.

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:03.629 line:0%
Jimmy and me --
our bar is way low."

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.031 line:0%
-Yeah. We're not bakers.

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.602 line:0%
We'll never be a baker.
-No, we're not going to be.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.304 line:0%
-But I was happy.
I've never made it.

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.506 line:0%
I was actually happy
that I knew how to braid.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:14.774 line:0%
And you taught me -- It was --

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.243
And the sesame seed I put on.
It was just great.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:20.747
I loved it. Look at that.
Mine's a little basic.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:23.616
Alright, alright. Take that off.
Okay, we got you.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.218 line:0%
Mine's a little unprofessional.
-Memories.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:27.220 line:0%
Are you still baking?
-I try to.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.256 line:0%
Whenever I can, I try to cook.
Do you do a lot of cooking?

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:31.590 line:0%
-I like cooking.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.560 line:0%
I was brought up --
So, my mom and dad, both cooks.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.661 line:0%
Both really different.

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:37.363 line:0%
Like, my dad --
classic accountant --

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.532 line:0%
followed the recipe exactly,

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.835 line:0%
and so it'll come out exactly
as the recipe says.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:44.036 line:0%
My mom has
never wrote down a recipe.

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:46.672 line:0%
She just cooks.
She's like an artist.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:50.309 line:0%
In fact, I have to show you
this thing -- I have it here --

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:52.878 line:0%
because it's one of the
greatest gifts I've ever gotten.

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:56.649 line:0%
So, my mom wrote this book for
me for my birthday,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:58.918 line:0%
and inside --
look how neat that is --

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:02.622 line:0%
handwritten,
every recipe I grew up with.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.524 line:0%
-Oh!
-Right? So all my favorites.

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:06.693 line:0%
And I said, "Mom,
but how did you get the recipes?

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:09.528 line:0%
Because you don't
go for recipes."

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:12.231 line:0%
And she goes, "I made them all."
I said, "What?"

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.499 line:0%
She said, "Yeah.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:16.302 line:0%
Over the last year,
I've had to make everything

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:19.305 line:0%
from your childhood
and write down

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.173 line:0%
and measure and do all that."

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:23.642 line:0%
Is that just not the great --
Like, I immediately

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:25.311 line:0%
felt like a pretty crap parent.

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:26.445 line:0%
I was like, "Wow."

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:29.148 line:0%
So my presents --
they've got a long ways --

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:30.283 line:0%
-I know. Exactly.

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:31.918 line:0%
Yeah, you're like,
"How do I top that one?"

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:36.655 line:0%
Wait. I did something similar
with my mom.

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:39.525 line:0%
You know, my mom's passed away
now, a few years now,

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:43.262 line:0%
and I know -- But before that,
I gave her this book,

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:45.298 line:0%
and it said "Grandma's favorite
recipes" or something.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.800 line:0%
I go, "I know it says 'grandma,'
but ignore that."

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.569 line:0%
I go, "And just write down --

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.337 line:0%
Just when you have the time,
if you have a recipe

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:52.738 line:0%
for what I used to like
during the holidays."

00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:55.174 line:0%
And she was like,
"I'm not a grandma.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.143 line:0%
I didn't even have grandkids.
I'm not a grandma."

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:58.611 line:0%
I go, "I understand.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:01.080 line:0%
You're young, and that's the
whole thing and we understand."

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.348 line:0%
But I made her do this thing and
I kept checking on her like,

00:03:03.348 --> 00:03:04.884 line:0%
"Hey, is it almost done?"

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.852 line:0%
And then, one Christmas,
she gave it to me.

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:08.922 line:0%
She goes,
"I finally finished that book."

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:11.824 line:0%
And it is magic,
because, like you said,

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.893 line:0%
there's no real recipes.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.995 line:0%
They're like a
pinch of something.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.130 line:0%
-Yeah, your whole life, you go,

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.267 line:0%
"Oh, but my mom's crème caramel,
or I wish I could have that."

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.470 line:0%
And, so, I made like
8 out of 10 versions

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.374 line:0%
of everything my mom cooked me
when I was growing up.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:31.444 line:0%
-That is magic.
-It's just quotes all through.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:35.114 line:0%
Julia Child -- "If you're scared
about it, try cream."

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:38.017 line:0%
Like, all this great stuff.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.019 line:0%
-Dude, I have to get out
my mom's cookbook,

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.888 line:0%
because it's similar
and it's the best thing.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.023 line:0%
It just --
It makes you think of mom,

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.291 line:0%
and you just -- I love it.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:47.393 line:0%
And I think that's
the secret to cooking, right?

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:50.362 line:0%
Putting the love into it anyway.
-You got it.

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:53.499 line:0%
-Yeah. Last time, we talked
about "The Music Man,"

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:56.602 line:0%
and since then, obviously,
Broadway closed.

00:03:56.602 --> 00:03:58.604 line:0%
"Music Man"
postponed till spring,

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:00.806 line:0%
but it's still happening, right?

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:03.443 line:0%
-100%. We go into
rehearsals in February,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:05.745 line:0%
and then we'll open in May.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:09.448 line:0%
-Wow. And how do you keep
yourself ready?

00:04:09.448 --> 00:04:12.151 line:0%
-I sing every day,
drive my kids crazy.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:15.555 line:0%
I'm up there singing every day.
I do scene lessons every week.

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:19.125 line:0%
I do dance lessons
with my, like, choreographer.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.461 line:0%
Sometimes, I do, like,
the Broadway Dance Center,

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:27.300 line:0%
you know, just the $12.95.
which is sometimes --

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.202 line:0%
I've got to say, sometimes,
it's humiliating,

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:32.572 line:0%
because there will be like
70 people in the class,

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:35.875 line:0%
and I don't have my video
on, obviously,

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.844 line:0%
but I do look,
and it's just basically

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:42.648 line:0%
68 of them
are teenage girls and me.

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:44.817 line:0%
And I think, "Is this weird?
This is weird."

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.719 line:0%
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:48.354 line:0%
But I do that occasionally.
Then I sing.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:53.059 line:0%
I get with Patrick Vaccariello,
my MD,

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:54.326 line:0%
and we'll just sing
through the theme,

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:55.661 line:0%
because I'm a working man.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.230 line:0%
I'm like you. I'm a worker.
-Yeah.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.864 line:0%
-I like to work.
-You're a perfectionist.

00:04:58.864 --> 00:05:01.633 line:0%
You always show up, and when you
do, it's a great job.

00:05:01.633 --> 00:05:04.269 line:0%
I was actually looking at
a bunch of your --

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:07.439 line:0%
I went in a deep dive and found
some old head shots of yours.

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:09.508 line:0%
I thought it would be kind of
fun to maybe take a look

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:12.611 line:0%
and just say, like, what were
you going through at that time.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.546 line:0%
Does that sound okay?
-Okay. Yeah, let's do that.

00:05:14.546 --> 00:05:15.681 line:0%
-Alright, check this out.

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:19.786 line:0%
First up,
tell me about this guy.

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:22.622 line:0%
What year was this?
And what was he into?

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:27.393 line:0%
-1995. That was "Correlli."
The fake tats there.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.262 line:0%
This was my
first job that I did.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:31.196 line:0%
My wife was the star of it.

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:34.233 line:0%
And she used to say to
me, "Don't take the tats off.

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:36.836 line:0%
Take them home."

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:39.071 line:0%
But, yeah, that was about
a five-month job,

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:41.840 line:0%
and, so, we got together about
two or three months in.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.276 line:0%
And -- I don't know.

00:05:44.276 --> 00:05:47.346 line:0%
It does look
a little porn, that --

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.014 line:0%
Yeah, I didn't use that a lot.

00:05:49.014 --> 00:05:52.084 line:0%
-I tried to pull off
a kind of version of that,

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:53.619 line:0%
because I thought,
like, I was really --

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.288 line:0%
You know, I love Paul McCartney,
and I was in a phase where

00:05:56.288 --> 00:05:57.890 line:0%
I was looking at all of
his Wings stuff

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.058 line:0%
and how cool he looked.

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.293 line:0%
He kind of had that mullet,
but, you know,

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:02.361 line:0%
shorter, hair up top.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:04.263 line:0%
And I go,
"I'm going to do that."

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:07.232 line:0%
And it looks awful.
-Can I see it?

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:09.802 line:0%
-No. I actually banned it.
-Oh, come on.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.838 line:0%
Alright, if there's anyone
there, like, in the control room

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.974 line:0%
who's stashed
some -- I just want to see it.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.009 line:0%
If there's anything there.
I'm just saying.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:18.010 line:0%
-No, that's-
-Hey!

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.645 line:0%
-No, that's just my --
-Hey!

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:22.214 line:0%
-That's just my first head shot.
That's what that one was.

00:06:22.214 --> 00:06:24.416 line:0%
How do you have that
lying around?

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:26.351 line:0%
That, I paid --
That was next to, like,

00:06:26.351 --> 00:06:27.586 line:0%
the local hardware store.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:29.822 line:0%
Some guy was like, "Yeah,
we also do head shots."

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:33.292 line:0%
And what role
was I going for there?

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.260 line:0%
-Looks like the center part.
Is that the center part?

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.163 line:0%
-Oh, that is such a center part.
That took me forever.

00:06:38.163 --> 00:06:40.099 line:0%
And who is going to
laugh at this comedian?

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:43.135 line:0%
He doesn't look funny at all.
-No, this is very dramatic.

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:44.269 line:0%
-He looks sad.
-Dramatic.

00:06:44.269 --> 00:06:46.138 line:0%
Yeah, it's very
Daniel Day-Lewis.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:48.941 line:0%
What year is this, Jimmy?

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:51.243 line:0%
-Get that off!
I can't even look at this.

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:54.447 line:0%
Let's look at this head shot of
you, this other one I found.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:59.418 line:0%
Oh, I like it. That's clean-cut.
-Yeah, that's

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.053 line:0%
Look at me,
the Golden retriever.

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:03.289 line:0%
That is, like,
me at drama school.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:06.158 line:0%
I'm like, "Yeah, I can do
a commercial for a bank.

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:08.227 line:0%
What do you want?"
-What do you guys want?

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:11.897 line:0%
I'll be right there. Sure.
Whatever you guys want.

00:07:11.897 --> 00:07:15.768 line:0%
-Being in drama school, everyone
else just seemed way cooler.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:18.971 line:0%
They all looked like, you know,
Jimmy Dean, Brando --

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:20.573 line:0%
leather jackets,
smoking cigarettes.

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:23.442 line:0%
And look at me.
I'm like a big golden retriever.

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:26.212 line:0%
Hi. This is so much fun.
I've got a monologue.

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.046 line:0%
Shall I do a monologue?

00:07:28.046 --> 00:07:29.515 line:0%
-I love it, though. It's great.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:33.052 line:0%
Well, last month marked 20 years

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:35.188 line:0%
since your debut as Wolverine
in "X-Men."

00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:36.622 line:0%
-Yeah.

00:07:36.622 --> 00:07:41.660 line:0%
-In fact, we found your original
Wolverine audition from 1999.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.763 line:0%
Let's take a look at this.
-Oh!

00:07:44.763 --> 00:07:48.434
-[ Chuckles ]

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:49.835
I'm not like you.

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:51.103
Listen, kid.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:52.838
About the only thing you've done
to endear yourself to me

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:53.839
is to get me into a fight.

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:56.742
So listen to me. Shut up.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.611
[ Imitates claw whooshing ]

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:00.779 line:0%
[ Both laugh ]
-[ Imitates claw whooshing ]

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:01.914 line:0%
-Dude...

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:04.182 line:0%
-A little more magician
than Wolverine.

00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:07.787 line:0%
-Now, did that human
know that you'd be playing

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:11.022 line:0%
that role for the next 17 years?

00:08:11.022 --> 00:08:16.062 line:0%
-No. I -- By the way,
when I walked into that room,

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:18.798 line:0%
I was pretty sure that
I wasn't playing the role.

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:20.867 line:0%
It was like a --
It was a weird audition,

00:08:20.867 --> 00:08:22.368 line:0%
because Dougray Scott
had the role,

00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:24.804 line:0%
and then he got caught up on
"Mission: Impossible 2,"

00:08:24.804 --> 00:08:26.672 line:0%
but everyone thought
that would get sorted.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:29.408 line:0%
But they said, "Look, we need a
backup plan just in case."

00:08:29.408 --> 00:08:31.943 line:0%
So it was like this Hail Mary
to begin with,

00:08:31.943 --> 00:08:33.579 line:0%
which is probably
the best way to do an audition.

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:35.548 line:0%
You're going in like, "Eh."

00:08:35.548 --> 00:08:38.450 line:0%
And I remember thinking,
"Alright, so I'm going in there.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.720 line:0%
Literally,
you showed a clip of about --

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.387 line:0%
That was -- How long was that?
15 seconds?

00:08:42.387 --> 00:08:45.190 line:0%
I think the entire audition
was about 20 seconds.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.959 line:0%
It wasn't much longer.
-Wow!

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:49.594 line:0%
-And -- Yeah, they were actually
filming at that time.

00:08:49.594 --> 00:08:50.962 line:0%
I went up -- It was in Toronto.

00:08:50.962 --> 00:08:54.099 line:0%
That was in one of the offices,
and it was at lunchtime.

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:55.768 line:0%
So, first of all,
I never thought

00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:57.736 line:0%
anything was happening
with that.

00:08:57.736 --> 00:09:00.940 line:0%
And I flew back that next day,
and Kevin Feige took me --

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.009 line:0%
He drove me to the airport,
who now runs Marvel,

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:05.144 line:0%
for those who don't know him.

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:07.512 line:0%
He was one of
the assistants there.

00:09:07.512 --> 00:09:08.681 line:0%
And I said,
"Dude, you don't have to --

00:09:08.681 --> 00:09:09.915 line:0%
Everyone knows
I'm not getting the part.

00:09:09.915 --> 00:09:11.917 line:0%
Like, you don't have to drive me
to the airport."

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.352 line:0%
And he goes,
"No, I'll drive you,

00:09:13.352 --> 00:09:15.420 line:0%
and we'll stop,
grab some dinner."

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.888 line:0%
I was like, "Dude,
you don't have to be that nice.

00:09:16.888 --> 00:09:19.291 line:0%
Like, thank you. We all get it.
We all understand."

00:09:19.291 --> 00:09:23.061 line:0%
So, who would have thought,
nine years later --

00:09:23.061 --> 00:09:24.897 line:0%
nine movies later,
20 years later,

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.332 line:0%
that that would have happened.

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:29.301 line:0%
I mean, honestly, in Hollywood,
a mate of mine said to me,

00:09:29.301 --> 00:09:30.736 line:0%
who's quite high up
in the business,

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:32.638 line:0%
and he said,
"Dude, the word on the street

00:09:32.638 --> 00:09:35.007 line:0%
is not good about the movie.

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:36.675 line:0%
No one watches
comic-book movies.

00:09:36.675 --> 00:09:40.245 line:0%
They're, like, dead.
That thing's gone forever.

00:09:40.245 --> 00:09:42.114 line:0%
Book another movie
before it comes out.

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:44.516 line:0%
Like, just say you're the lead
in a big movie

00:09:44.516 --> 00:09:45.684 line:0%
and try and book another movie.

00:09:45.684 --> 00:09:47.820 line:0%
That way, at least
you'll get another shot."

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.988 line:0%
So I did.

00:09:48.988 --> 00:09:51.223 line:0%
I was just trying to get
an audition, you know.

00:09:51.223 --> 00:09:53.159 line:0%
-No way. I love those stories.

00:09:53.159 --> 00:09:55.961 line:0%
-No one understood it was
the beginning of --

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:58.797 line:0%
Like, Comic-Con was
a 50,000-person thing.

00:09:58.797 --> 00:10:00.398 line:0%
The Internet
was really just beginning.

00:10:00.398 --> 00:10:03.068 line:0%
No one really understood.

00:10:03.068 --> 00:10:06.105 line:0%
They thought -- What they
thought was, like, a subculture.

00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:07.472 line:0%
Comic books
was actually mainstream,

00:10:07.472 --> 00:10:08.474 line:0%
but no one knew that.

00:10:08.474 --> 00:10:09.575 line:0%
-Gosh!

